\textbf{1.3} \textbf{Software Development Methodologies and Methods}

In this section we are going to present \textbf{SCRUM} and
\textbf{CRISP-DM} the 2 methodologies that we adopted for this project
and why we adopted this specific methodologies.

\textbf{1.3.1} \textbf{Scrum Method}

Scrum methodology represents a widely adopted framework for effectively
managing intricate projects, particularly prevalent within the domain of
software engineering. Funda-mentally, Scrum fosters a collaborative
approach to work, underscoring the importance of col-lective effort,
transparency, and adaptability. In contrast to conventional project
management approaches that depend on extensive preliminary planning,
Scrum operates on principles of iterative development and continuous
refinement {[}1{]}.

Within the Scrum framework, work is structured into brief, time-boxed
cycles referred During each sprint, the team to as "sprints," typically
spanning from one to four weeks.

concentrates on producing a small, demonstrable segment of the overall
project, known as a functional increment. Regular meetings, such as
daily stand-ups and sprint reviews, are held to maintain team alignment
and monitor progress. By segmenting work into manageable units and
prioritizing flexibility and responsiveness, Scrum empowers teams to
rapidly adjust to evolving requirements and deliver high-quality
products efficiently {[}1{]}.

At its core, Scrum endeavors to satisfy client needs through open
communication, shared accountability, and a dedication to ongoing
enhancement. Starting with a broad project con-cept, this vision is
progressively refined into a prioritized list of functionalities (termed
the product backlog) that align with the product owner's objectives.
This agile methodology has become a foundational practice in software
development, fostering innovation and client satis-faction {[}1{]}.
\end{quote}

6

\begin{quote}
Chapter 1. Project Study
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=3.34583in,height=2.23056in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image5.png}

\textbf{Figure 1.3:} Scrum-process

\begin{quote}
Scrum is, precisely, an evolution stemming from Agile Management
principles. The Scrum methodology is built upon a set of clearly defined
practices and roles that are integral throughout the software
development lifecycle. It is a highly adaptable methodology that
encourages the application of the 12 agile principles within a context
mutually agreed upon by all members of the product team {[}1{]}.

Scrum is executed in temporary, short, and recurring intervals, known as
Sprints, which typically last between 2 to 4 weeks, serving as periods
for feedback and reflection. Each Sprint functions as a self-contained
unit, meaning it yields a complete outcome---a version of the final
product that should be ready for delivery to the client with minimal
effort upon request {[}1{]}.

The process commences with a compilation of objectives and requirements
that collec-tively form the
https://www.nimblework.com/blog/project-planning/project plan. The
project's client is responsible for prioritizing these objectives,
balancing their perceived value against their associated cost, which in
turn dictates the sequence of iterations and subsequent deliveries
{[}1{]}.

Modern markets demand high quality, rapid delivery, and reduced costs.
To meet these demands, companies must exhibit significant agility and
flexibility in product development, striving for short development
cycles that can satisfy customer needs without compromising the quality
of the end result. Scrum is recognized as a very straightforward
methodology to implement and has gained considerable popularity due to
the quick results it consistently achieves {[}1{]}.

While Scrum methodology is predominantly employed in software
development, its ad-vantages are increasingly being recognized and
adopted by other sectors, which are integrating this framework into
their organizational structures, including sales, marketing, and HR
teams, among others {[}1{]}.
\end{quote}

