\begin{quote}
This stage involves the collection, exploration, and assessment of all
available data per-tinent to the project. The data science team
meticulously analyzes the data to comprehend its structure, evaluate its
quality, identify potential issues, determine its relevance, and
con-firm its availability {[}2{]}. This process helps in pinpointing
missing values or inconsistencies that could negatively impact
subsequent analyses. Subsequently, the team works with domain ex-perts
to devise strategies for resolving identified problems and to assist in
interpreting the data effectively {[}2{]}.

\textbf{1.3.2.3 Data preparation}

Once the data has been thoroughly evaluated, the next step is to select
the most pertinent variables and data samples for analysis, guided by
the predefined project objectives {[}2{]}.

The chosen data is then cleaned and, if necessary, transformed. This can
encompass addressing missing values, sampling data subsets, and
generating new, derived variables. If multiple data sources are being
utilized, they are integrated to form a cohesive dataset {[}2{]}. This
particular step frequently consumes more than half of the total project
time.

\textbf{1.3.2.4 Modeling}

In this phase, the data science team constructs predictive or
descriptive models, utilizing various data analysis techniques,
depending on the specific aims of the project. Multiple itera-tions
often occur between the data preparation and modeling stages to
fine-tune the application of particular algorithms {[}2{]}.

The modeling stage typically yields several distinct Data Mining models,
all designed to address the same core problem {[}2{]}.

\textbf{1.3.2.5 Evaluation}

Upon the completion of model construction, they undergo rigorous
evaluation to ascer-tain their quality and accuracy. This cyclical step
ensures that the developed model aligns with the project's stated
objectives {[}2{]}.

Model performance is quantified using appropriate metrics and compared
against the success criteria established in the initial phase of the
cycle. If the results do not meet expec-tations or if deficiencies are
identified, previous steps can be revisited and repeated to enhance
model performance {[}2{]}.
\end{quote}

