\end{quote}

\textbf{Figure 2.6:} A kernel acts like a filter on an image

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.6 demonstrates how a Kernel, when applied to an image,
functions as a filter, thereby modifying the image. CNNs are designed to
adjust the values within these kernels during training to optimize
performance for a specific task {[}4{]}.

The actual filtering process involves moving the kernel systematically
across different sections of an image. At each position, the kernel's
values are multiplied by the corresponding image pixel values and then
summed to produce a new output value. This systematic move-ment is why
CNNs are named as they are; in mathematics, this sweeping action is
termed "convolving" {[}4{]}.

\includegraphics[width=5.35417in,height=2.33333in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image15.png}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Figure 2.7:} The process of Sweeping

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.7 shows the process of the sweeping or convolving over an
image.

For computer vision applications, CNNs typically employ a sequence of
convolution and information compression steps to transform an image into
a compact and meaningful representation. This condensed representation
is then utilized by a traditional neural network to accomplish the final
objective {[}4{]}.

The most common method a CNN uses to compress an image into a useful
representation is through "max pooling." This technique involves
dividing an image into NN squares and then retaining only the highest
value from each of these squares {[}4{]}.
\end{quote}

