\begin{quote}
Prior to the advent of methods like YOLO, the "sliding window" approach
was the primary strategy for object detection. Object detection aims to
identify instances of specific objects within an image. In the sliding
window technique, a window is moved across an image, and a
classification model then categorizes the content within that window
{[}4{]}.

\includegraphics[width=4.68472in,height=2.63611in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image18.png}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Figure 2.10:} Sliding Window Process

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.10 conceptually demonstrates the process of classifying various
windows as containing a dog or not. A classification model, trained to
determine if an image contains a dog, is presented with several
"windows" from an image. By recording the windows the model identifies
as containing a dog, we can establish a set of windows that likely
contain a dog {[}4{]}.

\includegraphics[width=6.02361in,height=1.59167in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image19.png}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Figure 2.11:} Bounding box application on the image

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.11 shows that once several classifications have been made via
sliding window, an overall bounding box can be calculated. In this
example, we simply found the bounding box which includes all windows
where the model predicted a presence of a dog {[}4{]}.
\end{quote}

