\begin{quote}
Instead of arbitrarily moving a window across an image, selective search
aims to identify more effective windows based on the image's inherent
content. This method first locates small regions within an image that
share similar pixel characteristics. Subsequently, similar adjacent
regions are progressively merged to form larger areas. These larger
regions can then be used to propose potential bounding boxes {[}4{]}.

\includegraphics[width=4.68472in,height=3.00556in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image20.png}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Figure 2.12:} Selective Search

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.12 demonstrates that with selective search, bounding boxes are
suggested by the image content itself, rather than by random sweeping.
Several approaches have leveraged selective search to significantly
enhance object detection performance {[}4{]}.

One of the most notable models to employ this technique is R-CNN, which
utilized a specially trained convolutional network on these proposed
regions to achieve high-quality object detection {[}4{]}.

\includegraphics[width=5.35417in,height=1.5375in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image21.png}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Figure 2.13:} R-CNN Diagram

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.13 presents a diagram from the R-CNN paper, showing its use of
selective search
\end{quote}

