R-CNN was a dominant force in computer vision for a considerable period,
leading to numerous derivative concepts. However, it remained
computationally intensive.\\
YOLO revolutionized the R-CNN paradigm, introducing a fundamentally new
approach to image processing that continues to be highly relevant today
{[}4{]}.

\textbf{2.3} \textbf{YOLO Model}

The core concept behind YOLO is to perform all object detection tasks in
a single pass through a CNN, hence its name, "You Only Look Once." This
means a single CNN must, in one inference, identify multiple objects,
classify them correctly, and draw their bounding boxes {[}4{]}.

\includegraphics[width=4.68472in,height=1.93889in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image22.png}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Figure 2.14:} YOLO in action

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.14 represents an example of YOLO in action.

To achieve this, the creators of YOLO divided the object detection
problem into two sub-tasks and designed a model to execute both
simultaneously {[}4{]}.

\textbf{2.3.1} \textbf{Regionalized Classification}

YOLO partitions images into an arbitrary number of regions and then
classifies all these regions concurrently. It accomplishes this by
altering the output architecture of a standard CNN {[}4{]}.

Typically, a CNN compresses an image into a compact 2D representation,
which is then "flattened" into a single vector. This vector is
subsequently fed into a dense network to generate a classification
{[}4{]}.
\end{quote}

