\textbf{Figure 2.15:} CNN predictions

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.15 illustrates a conceptual diagram of how a conventional CNN
might predict the presence or absence of a dog in an image. Convolution
and max pooling generate a dense image representation, which is then
flattened and processed by a neural network {[}4{]}.

In contrast to this traditional approach, YOLO predicts classes for
sub-regions of the image rather than for the entire image {[}4{]}.

In YOLO, the convolutional output is flattened as usual, but then it's
transformed back into a 2D representation of shape S x S x C. Here, S
denotes the granularity of image subdivision into regions, and C
represents the number of classes being predicted. Both S and C are
adjustable parameters that allow YOLO to be adapted for different tasks
{[}4{]}.

\includegraphics[width=6.02361in,height=0.99722in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image24.png}
