\textbf{Figure 2.19:} CNN Perspective field example

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.19 demonstrates the perceptive field problem. Due to the
inherent nature of CNNs, the final dense representation contains
information specific to particular regions. This concept is commonly
referred to as a "perceptive field," as, for instance, the top-left
portion of the dense representation can only "perceive" (contain
information based on) the top-left section of the input image {[}4{]}.

However, YOLO processes the dense 2D representation through a fully
connected neural network, allowing information from each perceptive
field to interact before the final prediction is made {[}4{]}.
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=3.34722in,height=1.36806in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image28.png}

\textbf{Figure 2.20:} YOLO perspective field example

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.20 illustrates the YOLO perceptive field process in action.
While the dense representation from the CNN is region-specific, the fact
that the result is passed through a dense network means that the final
output for any given region is informed by data from all regions in the
image {[}4{]}.

This capability allows YOLO to consider the entire image before making
its final predic-tion, a crucial distinction that makes YOLO more robust
than R-CNN in terms of contextual awareness {[}4{]}.
\end{quote}

