\begin{quote}
In theory, we could use the predicted regions from the previous step to
draw bounding boxes, but the accuracy would be low. Furthermore, if two
dogs were positioned side-by-side, it would be impossible to
differentiate them from a single, wide dog, as both scenarios would
simply appear as a cluster of squares labeled "dog." To address these
issues, YOLO predicts bounding boxes in addition to class predictions
{[}4{]}.

YOLO assigns a "responsibility" to each square within the S x S grid.
Essentially, if a square contains the center of an object, then that
square is tasked with generating the bounding box for that specific
object {[}4{]}.

\includegraphics[width=5.35556in,height=1.95833in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image29.png}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Figure 2.21:} Responsability in YOLO

\begin{quote}
Figure 2.21 presents a conceptual diagram of "responsibility" in YOLO.
The SS square that encompasses the center of the dog is "responsible"
for the dog, and similarly for the bike and the car {[}4{]}.

The square responsible for a particular object is in charge of drawing
the bounding box for that object {[}4{]}.

In addition to the S x S x C tensor for class prediction (discussed
previously), YOLO also predicts an S x S x B x 5 tensor for bounding box
prediction. In this tensor, S again represents the image divisions, and
B denotes the number of bounding boxes each S x S square can generate.

Therefore, YOLO essentially generates multiple bounding boxes for each
square in the S x S grid, specifically B bounding boxes per square
{[}4{]}.
\end{quote}

