\begin{quote}
both safety and operational efficiency on the road {[}5{]}.

\textbf{Surveillance Systems:} In video surveillance, BEV assists in
overseeing expansive ar-eas by offering a complete overhead perspective
of the space. This viewpoint allows security personnel to track
movements, identify anomalies, and cover more ground with fewer cameras,
bolstering overall security and eliminating blind spots. BEV in
surveillance proves particularly advantageous in applications such as
smart city monitoring, airport security, and managing stadium events,
where clear situational awareness is paramount {[}5{]}.

\textbf{Augmented Reality:} For AR applications, BEV enriches user
immersion by facilitating interaction with environments from an overhead
perspective. This added spatial context is invaluable in sectors like
architecture, gaming, and urban planning, where users benefit from an
improved understanding of layouts and spatial relationships. BEV enables
users to switch between perspectives, boosting both the realism and
utility of AR experiences, ultimately improving user engagement and
comprehension {[}5{]}.

\textbf{2.4.3} \textbf{Bird's Eye View Implementation main concepts}

\textbf{2.4.3.1 Perspective Transformation}

The primary hurdle in generating a Bird's Eye View (BEV) involves
converting an im-age captured from a specific two-dimensional viewpoint
into a top-down representation. This conversion relies on intricate
mathematical operations known as projective transformations {[}5{]}.
\end{quote}

When a camera records a scene, it projects that scene onto its image
plane from its particular viewpoint. To convert this viewpoint into a
Bird's Eye perspective, a homography transformation is applied. This
process maps points from one plane to another, utilizing the principles
of projective geometry to "warp" the captured image and reconstruct the
scene as This transformation is indispensable in BEV applications if
observed directly from above.

\begin{quote}
because it re-establishes spatial relationships within the scene,
allowing systems to interpret depth, distance, and relative positioning
with high precision. Through these transformations, BEV augments machine
perception, making it crucial in fields such as autonomous navigation,
robotic path planning, and spatial analytics {[}5{]}.

\textbf{2.4.3.2 Homography}

In computer vision, the \textbf{homography matrix (H)} is essential for
projective trans-formations between two planes. This matrix facilitates
the mapping of points from one plane (such as the camera's original
perspective) to another plane (like a top-down, Bird's Eye View),
\end{quote}

33

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 2\tabcolsep) * \real{0.5000}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 2\tabcolsep) * \real{0.5000}}@{}}
