\begin{quote}
Once the homography matrix is computed, you can transform the original
image into the Bird's Eye View by applying the matrix transformation to
each pixel. Many computer vision libraries, such as OpenCV, offer
functions to simplify this warping operation. For instance, the
warpPerspective function in OpenCV facilitates this transformation:\\
bird\emph{eyeview} = \emph{cv}2\emph{.warpPerspective}(\emph{image,
homographymatrix, outputsize}){[}5{]}\emph{.}

\textbf{2.4.4.4 Refinement}

After the image warping, some post-processing might be necessary to
correct any dis-tortions or artifacts resulting from the transformation.
Techniques like edge detection, filtering, and contrast adjustment can
improve the clarity and quality of the final Bird's Eye View {[}5{]}.

\textbf{2.4.5} \textbf{Person Detection and Mapping in Bird's Eye View}

Once generated, the Bird's Eye View provides an optimal input for person
detection algorithms, allowing for the identification and localization
of individuals from an overhead perspective within the store
environment. This top-down view significantly boosts the precision and
robustness of person tracking, minimizing issues related to occlusion
and varied viewpoints common with traditional camera angles. By mapping
the detected individuals in the BEV space, the system can offer valuable
insights into customer movement patterns, occupancy levels in different
areas of the store, and potential congestion points. This information is
critical for optimizing store layout, enhancing customer flow, and
improving the overall shopping experience {[}5{]}.

\textbf{Conclusion}

This chapter reviewed the \textbf{state of the art} in computer vision,
crucial for our intelligent store monitoring system. We covered
\textbf{Computer Vision} basics, \textbf{CNNs}, and the efficient
\textbf{YOLO model} for object detection. We also introduced
\textbf{Bird's Eye View (BEV)}. These insights will guide our system's
development, providing real-time store management solutions.
\end{quote}

