\begin{quote}
Chapter 3. Sprint 0 - Requirements identification

\textbf{Introduction}

This chapter outlines the initial phase of our project. We'll start by
defining the \textbf{Scrum team roles}. Then, we'll identify both the
\textbf{functional and non-functional requirements} for our intelligent
store monitoring system, detailing how data will be handled and
presented. Finally, we'll present a \textbf{use case diagram} and the
\textbf{product backlog} to guide our development.

\textbf{3.1} \textbf{Role Identification}

The scrum team represented in the figure 3.1 is composed of : Scrum
master: Nassim Hammemi\\
Developer: Mohamed Yahya Gadhgadhi
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=3.34583in,height=1.51389in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image39.png}

\textbf{Figure 3.1:} Scrum Team

\begin{quote}
\textbf{3.2} \textbf{Requirements Identification}

\textbf{3.2.1} \textbf{Functional requirements identification}

The Functional requirements of our project are the following: \textbf{1.
Data Extraction:}

• \textbf{Video Stream Ingestion:} The system shall ingest live or
recorded video streams as the primary source of raw data for analysis.

• \textbf{Person Detection and Tracking:} The system shall employ
computer vision models (e.g., object detection and tracking algorithms)
to identify and continuously track indi-vidual people within the video
frames. This includes generating unique identifiers for each tracked
person.

• \textbf{Spatial Coordinate Extraction:} For each tracked person, the
system shall extract their precise spatial coordinates (e.g., pixel
coordinates or transformed real-world coordinates) within the store
layout for every frame they are present.
\end{quote}

37

\begin{quote}
Chapter 3. Sprint 0 - Requirements identification

• \textbf{Demographic Feature Extraction:} The system shall utilize
computer vision models (e.g., classification models) to extract
demographic features, specifically gender, for each detected individual
from the video stream.

• \textbf{Perspective Transformation Data Acquisition:} The system shall
acquire and apply a perspective transformation matrix to convert pixel
coordinates from the video frames into real-world coordinates on the
store map, ensuring accurate spatial analysis.

• \textbf{Store Layout Information Input:} The system shall accept
definitions of the store map, including the boundaries of the retail
space and the precise locations and dimensions of predefined shelf
zones.

\textbf{2. Data Analysis:}

• \textbf{Customer Movement Path Construction:} The system shall
construct complete movement paths for each customer by concatenating
their extracted spatial coordinates over time.

• \textbf{Heatmap Generation:} The system shall generate a visual
representation (heatmap) of customer density across the store layout,
highlighting areas of high and low traffic, derived from aggregated
customer movement paths.

\textbf{-- Heatmap Smoothing:} The system shall smooth the raw heatmap
data to provide a clearer and more intuitive visual representation of
density.

\textbf{-- Heatmap Normalization:} The system shall normalize the
heatmap data to en- sure consistent visual representation across
different time periods or datasets.

• \textbf{Shelf Zone Interaction Detection:} The system shall identify
when customers are within the defined interaction radius of predefined
shelf zones and quantify the exact duration of these interactions.

• \textbf{Engagement Classification:} The system shall classify a
customer's interaction with a shelf as "engaged" if the time spent
within the interaction radius exceeds a predefined threshold.

• \textbf{Shelf Performance Metric Calculation:} The system shall
calculate key performance indicators for each shelf, including:

\textbf{--} The total number of unique \textbf{visitors}.
\end{quote}

38

\begin{quote}
Chapter 3. Sprint 0 - Requirements identification

\textbf{--} The number of \textbf{engaged visitors}.

\textbf{--} The \textbf{engagement rate} (proportion of visitors who
become engaged).
\end{quote}

\textbf{--} The \textbf{traffic share} (proportion of overall store
traffic that visits the shelf).

\begin{quote}
\textbf{--} A \textbf{simplified conversion rate} indicating the shelf's
ability to attract and engage traffic.

\textbf{--} A \textbf{composite performance score} for ranking shelves
based on multiple metrics.

• \textbf{Shelf Categorization:} The system shall categorize each
shelf's performance as "High Performing," "Average," or
"Underperforming" based on its calculated performance score and
predefined thresholds.

• \textbf{Overall People Tracking Statistics:} The system shall provide
high-level statistics about customer traffic volume and visit duration
for the entire store, including:

\textbf{--} The \textbf{total number of unique individuals} tracked
within the store during the analysis period.

\textbf{--} The \textbf{average time spent} per customer within the
store.

\textbf{--} Analysis of the \textbf{distribution of visit durations}
(e.g., identifying typical visit times, and ranges through percentiles).

• \textbf{Customer Demographic Distribution:} The system shall analyze
the extracted de- mographic features to present the overall
\textbf{gender distribution} of store visitors.

\textbf{3. UI Interface:}

• \textbf{Analysis Initiation Control:} The user interface shall provide
a clear and accessible control (e.g., a button) for the manager to
initiate the entire data processing and analysis workflow.

• \textbf{Report Download Functionality:} The user interface shall
provide a clear and acces-sible control (e.g., a button) for the manager
to generate and download a comprehensive report of the analysis
findings.

• \textbf{Visual Display of Heatmaps:} The user interface, or the
generated report, shall visually display the smoothed and normalized
heatmap, providing an intuitive representation of customer density.
\end{quote}

39

\begin{quote}
Chapter 3. Sprint 0 - Requirements identification

• \textbf{Shelf Performance Reporting:} The user interface, or the
generated report, shall present the calculated performance metrics and
categories for each shelf in an under-standable and organized format
(e.g., tables).

• \textbf{People Tracking Statistics Display:} The user interface, or
the generated report, shall display the overall customer traffic
statistics and the distribution of time spent in the store (e.g.,
through a histogram or key metrics).

• \textbf{Comprehensive Report Generation:} The system shall generate a
user-friendly, down-loadable document (e.g., a PDF report) that
summarizes all analysis results. This report shall include:

\textbf{--} An executive summary and report-specific details (like
generation date).

\textbf{--} Clearly organized sections with descriptive headings (e.g.,
Heatmap Analysis, Shelf Performance, People Tracking, Customer
Demographics).

\textbf{--} Embedded visual representations (such as heatmaps and visit
duration charts).

\textbf{--} Structured data presentations (e.g., tables for detailed
shelf metrics).

\textbf{--} Key textual insights and numerical summaries of the analysis
findings.

\textbf{3.2.2} \textbf{Nonfunctional Requirements Identification}

\textbf{1. Performance}

• \textbf{Analysis Speed:} The system shall complete the "Run analysis"
process within an ac-ceptable timeframe, considering the volume of input
data (process a typical day's worth of data in under 15 minutes).

• \textbf{Report Generation Speed:} The system shall generate the PDF
report within an acceptable timeframe (in under 10 seconds after
clicking "Download Report").

\textbf{2. Usability}

• \textbf{Intuitive Dashboard:} The dashboard interface shall be
intuitive and easy for a manager to navigate and understand the
presented insights.

• \textbf{Clear Visualizations:} Visualizations (heatmaps, histograms)
shall be clear, easy to interpret, and effectively convey the underlying
data.
\end{quote}

40

\begin{quote}
Chapter 3. Sprint 0 - Requirements identification

• \textbf{Comprehensive Report:} The generated PDF report shall be
comprehensive, well- organized, and provide all necessary information
for offline review.

\textbf{3. Reliability}

• \textbf{Data Accuracy:} The system shall accurately process raw data
and calculate metrics with high precision.

• \textbf{Error Handling:} The system shall gracefully handle errors
during data loading, pro- cessing, and report generation, providing
informative messages to the user.

• \textbf{Robustness:} The system shall be robust enough to handle
varying input data quality and quantity without crashing.

\textbf{4. Scalability}

• \textbf{Data Volume:} The system shall be able to handle increasing
volumes of customer movement data and shelf definitions without
significant degradation in performance.

\textbf{5. Maintainability}

• \textbf{Code Modularity:} The codebase shall be modular and
well-structured to facilitate future enhancements, bug fixes, and
maintenance.

• \textbf{Documentation:} The system's internal logic and external
interfaces shall be adequately documented.

\textbf{3.2.3} \textbf{Global Use Case Diagram}

This use case diagram illustrates the functionality of the "Store
Analysis Dashboard" system from the perspective of its primary user, the
Manager. It outlines the different ways the Manager interacts with the
system to perform analysis and generate reports.

\textbf{System Boundary:}\\
\textbf{Store Analysis Dashboard:} This represents the scope of the
system being modeled.

It encompasses all the functionalities related to analyzing store data.
\textbf{Actor:}\\
\textbf{Manager:} This is the main user of the system. The Manager
interacts with the Store Analysis Dashboard to access and utilize its
features. \textbf{Use Cases:}\\
The diagram depicts the following use cases, representing the specific
tasks the Manager can perform within the system:
\end{quote}

41

\begin{quote}
Chapter 3. Sprint 0 - Requirements identification

• \textbf{Authenticate:} The Manager must successfully authenticate
themselves. This ensures the system's security and restricts access to
authorized personnel.

• \textbf{Consult Heatmap Analysis:} This use case allows the Manager to
view and analyze heatmap data related to customer movement and activity
within the store. This is de-pendent on the "Store Analysis" being
performed.

• \textbf{Consult Shelfs Performance:} This use case enables the Manager
to access and review data related to the performance of different
shelves within the store.

• \textbf{Consult People Tracking:} This use case provides the Manager
with the ability to view data related to people time spent within the
store.

• \textbf{Consult Demography:} This use case allows the Manager to
consult demographic data related to store visitors or customers.

• \textbf{Download Report:} This use case represents the Manager's
action to initiate the down-load of a report. This use case is included
by the "Authenticate" use case, suggesting that report downloading is
available once the manager is authenticated.
\end{quote}

