\begin{quote}
Chapter 4. \textbf{Sprint 1 -- Customer Demography Identification}

\textbf{Introduction}

This chapter details our first development phase. We'll cover
\textbf{sprint planning} and the \textbf{sprint backlog}. The main focus
is on applying the \textbf{CRISP-DM methodology} to develop a
demographic identification model using \textbf{YOLOv11}. We'll explain
data preparation, model training, and evaluation, including the
post-processing logic for stable classifications. Finally, we'll present
the results and a sprint retrospective.

\textbf{4.1} \textbf{Application of CRISP-DM}

The development of the demographic identification model followed the
Cross-Industry Standard Process for Data Mining (CRISP-DM) methodology
within this sprint.

\textbf{4.1.1} \textbf{Business Understanding}

\textbf{-- Objective:} Automatically determine the demographic
composition (man, woman, child) of shop visitors to provide foundational
data for customer behavior analysis, targeted marketing, and optimizing
store layout.

\textbf{-- Success Criteria:} Achieve mAP of over 70\% on the tested
video with acceptable performance across all classes, and implement a
mechanism to provide a consistent demographic label per tracked
individual based on high-confidence predictions.

\textbf{4.1.2 Data Understanding}

\textbf{-- Source:} A custom dataset is required using images from
Roboflow Universe.

\textbf{-- Dataset Name:} Object detection dataset by Pinchuan.

\textbf{-- Link:} https://universe.roboflow.com/pinchuan/mk1501

\textbf{-- Content:} The dataset contains 7,200 images of full-body
persons captured from different angles.

\textbf{-- Classes:} The target classes are \emph{Male} and
\emph{Female}.

\textbf{-- Volume:}

Figure 4.1 presents a sample from the adopted dataset, which will
eventually be forked for use in this project.
\end{quote}

