\textbf{4.1.5 Evaluation}

The performance of the demography classification model was evaluated to
ensure the suit-ability for the subsequence of calssification tasks. The
evaluation comprised quantitative
\end{quote}

54

\begin{quote}
Chapter 4. \textbf{Sprint 1 -- Customer Demography Identification}

and qualitative results.

\textbf{4.1.5.1} \textbf{Methodology}

Quantitative evaluation was performed using standard object detection
metrics on the dedicated test split of the high-angle dataset. The
ultralytics framework's validation mode computed these metrics. The
primary criterion for selecting the best model weights was the highest
mAP@0.5: achieved on the validation set during training. Qualitative
assessment involved visually inspecting the model's detections on
various segments of the store video, paying particular attention to its
ability to handle the challenging top-down perspective, varying lighting
conditions, and instances of partial occlusion.

\textbf{4.1.5.2} \textbf{Quantitative Results}

The model was trained for 97 epochs. Based on the validation metrics
logged in results.csv, the epoch yielding the best mAP@0.5:0.95was
\textbf{Epoch 81} . For this epoch, the perfor-mance metrics for the
'male' and 'female' class (as this is a single-class detector, these are
the overall model metrics) are as follows:

\textbf{-- mAP@.5:.95 (mean Average Precision, IoU 0.5-0.95):} 0.78\%

\textbf{-- mAP@.5 (mean Average Precision, IoU 0.5):} 0.95\%

\textbf{-- Precision (P):} 0.89\%

\textbf{-- Recall (R):} 0.92\%

\textbf{4.1.5.3} \textbf{Qualitative Results}

Visual inspection of our gender classification model revealed that it
was indeed capable of performing its primary function of classifying
gender, establishing a baseline capability.

However, several limitations were qualitatively observed during testing.
Primarily, the model's performance was hindered by the relatively small
size of the training dataset. This constrained its ability to generalize
effectively, leading to instances where precision was not consistently
high. Qualitatively, this manifested as occasional misclassifications,
particularly in more ambiguous cases or with less common presentations
of gender, where the model might incorrectly identify individuals. There
were also observed instances where the model struggled to differentiate
between genders when visual cues were subtle or limited, suggesting a
lack of robust feature learning due to the data scarcity.
\end{quote}

55

\begin{quote}
Chapter 4. \textbf{Sprint 1 -- Customer Demography Identification}

Overall, while the model demonstrated foundational capabilities in
gender classification, its qualitative performance confirms that the
limited dataset size significantly impacted its precision, highlighting
the need for more extensive and diverse training data for improved
robustness and accuracy.

Figure 4.11 shows a screenshot of the model tested on the video.
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=6.02361in,height=3.40694in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image51.png}

\textbf{Figure 4.11:} Observation

\begin{quote}
\textbf{4.1.6 Post-Processing Logic for Classification Stability}

While the YOLOv11 model provides demographic classifications
(male/female) on a frame-by-frame basis, initial observations revealed
potential inconsistencies for the same individual across consecutive
frames, especially during entry or under varying angles pre-sented to
the entrance camera. To ensure a single, stable demographic
classification for each unique individual tracked by the system, a
post-processing decision logic was imple-mented. This logic operates on
the sequence of predictions generated for a person as they are observed
by the entrance camera.The algorithm Description is the following :

o For each tracked person ID appearing in the frame, the system attempts
to collect up to 29 consecutive frame classifications.

o Only classifications where the model's confidence score is \$
\textbackslash ge 0.7 \$ are considered valid and stored.
Classifications below this threshold are disregarded for the decision
process.

o Once the buffer (of up to 29 frames with confidence \$
\textbackslash ge 0.7 \$) is evaluated, the algorithm counts the
frequency of 'male' versus 'female' classifications within that buffer.
\end{quote}

56

\begin{quote}
Chapter 4. \textbf{Sprint 1 -- Customer Demography Identification}

o The demographic label ('male' or 'female') that occurs most frequently
is assigned as the definitive classification for that person ID for the
remainder of their tracked presence or for reporting purposes.

o If insufficient high-confidence frames are collected within the
29-frame window the window could slide until enough data is gathered.

\textbf{4.2} \textbf{Results}

The first sprint successfully established foundational capabilities for
customer demo-graphic identification, focusing on a gender
classification model. Key outputs include:

\textbf{-- Quantitative Performance:} The YOLOv11 model, trained for 97
epochs with Epoch 81 yielding the best mAP@0.5:0.95at 0.78\%,
demonstrated a mAP@0.5of 0.95\%. Precision (P) was 0.89\%, and Recall
(R) stood at 0.92\%. These metrics, while indicating a baseline
capability, also highlighted areas for improvement.

\textbf{-- Qualitative Observations:} Visual inspection confirmed the
model's ability to perform gender classification, establishing a working
prototype. However, the qual-itative assessment revealed limitations
primarily due to the relatively small dataset size. This led to
precision issues, manifesting as occasional misclassifications,
es-pecially in ambiguous cases or when visual cues were subtle,
indicating a struggle with generalization.

\textbf{-- Classification Stability:} A post-processing logic was
successfully implemented to ensure consistent demographic labels for
tracked individuals. This mechanism collects high-confidence
classifications over multiple frames and assigns the most frequent
label, significantly improving the stability of gender identification
for each person over time.

\textbf{4.3} \textbf{Sprint Retroprospective}

Sprint 1 achieved significant milestones in establishing the demographic
identification pipeline. The successful application of the
\textbf{CRISP-DM methodology} provided a struc-tured approach from
business understanding to evaluation. The detailed \textbf{data
prepa-ration process} , including forking, manual annotation, dataset
versioning with data splitting, transformations, and augmentation,
provides a crucial foundation for training.
\end{quote}

57

\begin{quote}
Chapter 4. \textbf{Sprint 1 -- Customer Demography Identification}

The selection of \textbf{YOLOv11} alongside the \textbf{PyTorch
framework} and \textbf{Google Colab environment with GPU T4
acceleration} proved effective for model training. Fur-thermore, the
development and integration of the \textbf{post-processing logic} for
classifica-tion stability addressed initial inconsistencies, ensuring
reliable demographic labeling for tracked individuals.

However, challenges were identified that will guide future development.
The primary limitation stemmed from the \textbf{dataset size} , which
qualitatively impacted the model's precision and generalization
capabilities, leading to observed misclassifications. Future sprints
will aim to address this by seeking larger and more diverse datasets to
enhance model robustness and accuracy in varied scenarios.

\textbf{Conclusion}

In \textbf{"Sprint 1,"} we successfully established our demographic
identification capabilities. Following \textbf{CRISP-DM}, we prepared
data and trained the \textbf{YOLOv11 model}. Our \textbf{post-processing
logic} ensured stable classifications, delivering valuable insights for
future analysis. This sprint provided crucial learnings, strengthening
our approach for upcoming development phases.
\end{quote}

