\begin{quote}
Chapter 5. Sprint 2 -- Customer Activity Mapping in store

\textbf{Introduction}

This chapter focuses on analyzing customer movement and engagement.
We'll outline our sprint goals and detail the \textbf{CRISP-DM
methodology} applied to overcome challenges in high-angle person
detection. This includes \textbf{data preparation} and fine-tuning a
\textbf{YOLO model}. We'll then explain how we transform video into a
\textbf{Bird's Eye View (BEV) map} and generate \textbf{heatmaps}.
Finally, we'll present our \textbf{results} and a
\textbf{retrospective}.

\textbf{5.1} \textbf{Application of CRISP-DM}

\textbf{5.1.1 Business Understanding}

\textbf{-- Technical challenge} : High angle camera view in store
introduces significant perspective distortion but the problem is that
pre-trained models like YOLO are trained on COCO dataset doesn't support
this angle unlike the standard frontal view. this geometric distortion
reduces the accuracy of person detection so this challenge requires
tailored dataset preparation and model fine-tuning specifically adapted
to the bird's-eye view.

\textbf{-- Need:} An accurate person detection m robust to the
high-angle perspective distor-tion present in the store's video feed.
This is fundamental for subsequent tracking and spatial analysis.

\textbf{-- Objective:} Fine-tune a YOLO model to reliably detect the
'person' class from the specific high-angle viewpoint, significantly
outperforming standard COCO pre-trained models in this context.

\textbf{-- Success Criteria:} Achieve a minimum mAP of 0.85 and Recall
of 0.80 for the 'per-son' class on a dedicated high-angle test set,
providing confidence in the detector's output for the tracking module.

\textbf{5.1.2 Data Understanding}

\textbf{-- Source:} Frames were extracted at intervals from the primary
video to create a dataset representative of the target environment. In
addition to that, an external dataset was forked to extend the dataset
to improve the model performance after training.
\end{quote}

