\textbf{5.1.5 Evaluation}

The performance of the person detection model was evaluated to ensure
the suitability for the subsequence of tracking and mapping tasks. The
evaluation comprised quantitative and qualitative results.

\textbf{5.1.5.1} \textbf{Methodology}

Quantitative evaluation was performed using standard object detection
metrics on the dedicated test split of the high-angle dataset. The
ultralytics framework's validation mode computed these metrics. The
primary criterion for selecting the best model weights was the highest
mAP@0.5: achieved on the validation set during training. Qualitative
assessment involved visually inspecting the model's detections on
various segments of the store video, paying particular attention to its
ability to handle the challenging top-down perspective, varying lighting
conditions, and instances of partial occlusion.

\textbf{5.1.5.2} \textbf{Quantitative Results}

The model was trained for 100 epochs. Based on the validation metrics
logged in results.csv, the epoch yielding the best mAP@0.5:0.95 was
\textbf{Epoch 86}. For this epoch, the performance metrics for the
'person' class (as this is a single-class detector, these are the
overall model metrics) are as follows:

\textbf{-- mAP@.5:.95 (mean Average Precision, IoU 0.5-0.95):}

\textbf{-- mAP@.5 (mean Average Precision, IoU 0.5):} 95.55\%

\textbf{-- Precision (P):} 94.42\%

\textbf{-- Recall (R):} 89.96\%

\textbf{5.1.5.3} \textbf{Qualitative Results}

Visual inspection of the model's performance on the video demonstrated a
significant improvement in detection reliability compared to initial
tests with standard pre-trained YOLO models. The \textbf{best.pt} model
successfully identified individuals viewed from the high angle,
effectively capturing the top-down profiles (heads and shoulders). It
showed
\end{quote}

