\begin{quote}
\textbf{5.2} \textbf{Process Logic}

\textbf{5.2.1 Key Points}

First step for making the heatmap is to identify key points in both the
desired to-down BEV map and the original store camera view. In this case
we used the first frame of the camera video to draw the desired BEV map
using shapes like rectangle and circle. The points will be saved once
and for all and will be eventually used to match with the points from
the real angle video.
\end{quote}

67

\begin{quote}
Chapter 5. Sprint 2 -- Customer Activity Mapping in store
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=3.34583in,height=2.17778in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image62.png}

\textbf{Figure 5.11:} Desired Map

\begin{quote}
Figure 5.11 represents the desired map drawing using the first frame of
the video so the map becomes more efficient and more practical.

After drawing the desired BEV Map, it is time to match the points from
the desired BEV map and the original store camera view. The key points
will be saved once and for all too so it can be used in making the
Homography matrix.
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=5.35417in,height=2.20972in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image63.png}

\textbf{Figure 5.12:} Matching Points

\begin{quote}
Figure 5.12 shows the process of the Key points matching between the BEV
map angle and the original camera angle.

\textbf{5.2.2 Homography Matrix}

This matrix encapsulates the geometric relationship between the two
views, allowing us to project points from the 2D camera image plane onto
the 2D BEV map plane. Once calculated, this matrix can be used to
transform any point in the camera view to its equivalent location in the
BEV map, which is crucial for accumulating data and generating the
heatmap.
\end{quote}

68

\begin{quote}
Chapter 5. Sprint 2 -- Customer Activity Mapping in store

The calculation of the Homography Matrix requires a set of corresponding
points that are visible in both the original camera view and the target
BEV map. As discussed in the previous section, these key points are
carefully selected and matched. A minimum of four non-collinear point
pairs are required to uniquely determine the 8 degrees of freedom of a
2D homography.

The code first extracts the corresponding saved point pairs previously
separating them into lists for the camera frame \textbf{frame points}
and the BEV map \textbf{map-points} . These lists are then converted
into NumPy arrays of type float32, which is the required input format
for OpenCV's findHomography function. A crucial check is performed to
ensure that at least four point pairs have been provided.

If this condition is met, the cv2.findHomography function is called.
This function is a core part of OpenCV used to find the perspective
transformation, or homography, between two planar objects. It takes sets
of corresponding points from the two images and estimates the
transformation matrix (H) that maps points from the first set to the
second. It implements an algorithm (commonly RANSAC or LMedS) to
robustly estimate this matrix, making it resilient to outliers in the
point correspondences.

The function takes frame-points (the source points from the camera
frame) and map-points (the destination points in the BEV map) as its
primary parameters to eventually return the calculated homography matrix
(H).Finally, the calculated Matrix is printed to the console and saved.
Saving the matrix allows it to be loaded and reused later for
transforming points without needing to re-select and match the key
points every time.

\textbf{5.2.3 BEV Projection}

Following the calculation of the Homography Matrix, the next critical
step is to apply this transformation in real-time (or near real-time) to
track objects (specifically people) in the original camera feed and
visualize their movement on the generated Bird's-Eye View (BEV) map.
This process involves several integrated components: object detection,
object tracking, and the application of the Homography Matrix for
perspective transfor-mation.

The Python script orchestrates these steps. It utilizes the previously
trained YOLO model for detecting persons from the top view angle of the
original camera video, the ByteTrack algorithm for robust object
tracking across frames, and the previously computed Homog-raphy Matrix
(H) to project the detected and tracked object locations onto the BEV
map.
\end{quote}

69

\begin{quote}
Chapter 5. Sprint 2 -- Customer Activity Mapping in store

The core logic of the script operates within a video processing loop.
For each frame (or a skipped frame to control the visualization speed),
the following sequence of operations is performed:

\textbf{1.Object Detection:} The frame is passed through the loaded YOLO
model (model(frame, ...)). The model predicts bounding boxes and
confidence scores for objects it detects, filtered\\
to only include the 'person'.

\textbf{2.Object Tracking:} The raw detections from the YOLO model are
then fed into the ByteTrack tracker
(tracker.update\_with\_detections(detections)). ByteTrack as-sociates
detections across consecutive frames, assigning a unique tracker\_id to
each distinct person and maintaining its track history. This is
essential for following individual people over time.

\textbf{3.Processing Tracked Persons:} The script iterates through the
tracked detections provided by ByteTrack. For each tracked person with a
valid tracker\_id:

\textbf{--} The ID is added to a set of active\_ids for the current
frame.

\textbf{--} A consistent color is assigned to the person based on their
ID if it's the first time seeing them.

\textbf{--} A bounding box and the assigned tracker\_id are drawn on a
copy of the original camera frame for visualization.

\textbf{--} The center point of the object's bounding box is calculated.
This point is typically used as the representative location of the
object for projection.

\textbf{--} The calculated center point from the original camera frame
is transformed to the BEV map coordinates using the Homography Matrix
via cv2.perspectiveTransform().

\textbf{--} The transformed point (in BEV coordinates) is stored as the
person's current posi- tion and appended to their movement path history
(people\_paths).

\textbf{4.Visualizing Movement on BEV Map:} After processing all tracked
objects in the current frame, the script iterates through the
active\_ids. For each active person:

\textbf{--} Their stored path history is retrieved. The path is
truncated to a fixed TRAIL\_LENGTH to keep the visualization manageable.

\textbf{--} The path (trail) is drawn on the live\_map. Line segments
connect consecutive points in the path, and an optional fading effect
(TRAIL\_FADE) is applied to make older points more transparent,
indicating the direction of movement.
\end{quote}

70

\begin{quote}
Chapter 5. Sprint 2 -- Customer Activity Mapping in store

\textbf{--} The person's current position (the latest point in their
path) is drawn as a larger circle on the live\_map, and their
tracker\_id is displayed nearby. \textbf{5.Saving:} Fi-nally, the
complete movement paths for all tracked individuals are saved to a file
(movement\_paths.pkl) using Python's pickle module for potential later
analysis or heatmap generation.
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=6.02361in,height=2.48611in]{vertopal_8745fb96beba4c7f9cea35a5bfa22ab3/media/image64.png}

\textbf{Figure 5.13:} BEV map activity

\begin{quote}
Figure 5.13 shows the customers activity inside the store with the
bird's ye view map.

\textbf{5.3} \textbf{Heatmap Generation}

The primary goal of heatmap generation is to create a visual
representation of aggregate customer traffic density and dwell time
across the store layout. This provides an intuitive overview of popular
and less-visited areas. The steps are as follows:

1. Initialization: The process begins with loading the saved movement
paths calculated earlier and initializing the accumulation grid of the
base map with zeros.

2. Accumulation: The process iterates through every coordinate point in
every saved path for each tracked person. For each point in a path, the
corresponding cell in the accumulation grid is incremented. This process
counts how many times a specific location in the BEV map was occupied by
a tracked person across all frames and all individuals, thereby
accumulating density values based on presence and dwell time.

3. Smoothing: The smoothing filter is applied to the accumulation grid
to blend high-density areas and reduce noise. The goal is to create a
visually smoother heatmap and to better represent the hot/cold areas.

4.Normalization: The smoothed accumulation grid is then normalized by
dividing its
\end{quote}

71

\begin{quote}
Chapter 5. Sprint 2 -- Customer Activity Mapping in store

values by the maximum value. This scales the intensity values to a range
suitable for consistent color mapping.

5.Color mapping: A custom colormap is applied to the normalized grid.
This colormap transitions from colors representing low intensity (like
blue) to colors representing high intensity (like red), creating a color
image representation of the heatmap.

6.Saving: The color heatmap is mixed the base map (including the
shelves) to create the result heatmap that will be saved to be analyzed
later.

\textbf{5.4} \textbf{Results}

The sprint successfully developed multi-stage pipeline for customer
activity analysis with this outputs:

\textbf{-- Qualitative observation} showed that the trained YOLO model
improved ability to detect persons with high angle perspective camera
providing a reliable foundation to the tracking aspect. This model
achived a precision of 94.42 percent for persons detection which
represents a high quantitative performance.

\textbf{-- The custom detector , ByteTrack} and the Homography
transformation were integrated into the processing pipeline assigning
unique ID's to detect individuals and recording their transformed
coordinates. This set of movementss were saved.

\textbf{--} Based on the saved data the customer activity heatmap was
generated and saved highlighting the activity of customers through
different areas of the store especially shelves.

\textbf{-- The interaction analysis} resumed the customer's activity
inside the store in this following metrics: Total people tracked, people
interacting with at least a shelf, shelves popularity and engagement
rates.

\textbf{5.5} \textbf{Sprint Retrospective}

Sprint 2 achieved significant milestones such as the successful
challenge of the trained YOLO model against the high angle camera
detections , the interactive bird's eye view map and the homography
transformation enabling accuracy of the perspective transfor-mation
without forgetting the integration with the tracker(ByteTrack) which
provided a high performance customer tracking system. Furthermore, the
heatmap visualizations
\end{quote}

72

\begin{quote}
Chapter 5. Sprint 2 -- Customer Activity Mapping in store

highlighted the customer's activity inside the store helping the
analysis about zones en-gagement and shelves interactions. This
demonstrates the sprint capability to deliver spatial analytics using a
well-structured pipeline.

However, the sprint identified future refinement features the manual map
drawing which can be enhanced by using the Store biano used by the
architect earlier for example also the detection model and tracker can
be improved for robustness in hightly complex scenarios like severe
occlusions or dense crowds. In addition the definition of the heatmap
generation like gaussian filter and shelves interactions could benefit
from more dynamic or sophisticated approaches.

\textbf{Conclusion}

In \textbf{"Sprint 2,"} we successfully moved to advanced customer
activity analysis. We fine-tuned a \textbf{YOLO model} for accurate
high-angle person detection and used \textbf{homography transformation}
to create a \textbf{BEV map} for precise customer tracking. This allowed
us to generate insightful \textbf{heatmaps}, providing valuable data to
optimize store layouts and enhance the customer experience. This sprint
significantly advanced our intelligent store management solution.
\end{quote}

