\textbf{6.3} \textbf{System Functionalities}

This section describes the main functionalities of the Store Analysis
Dashboard system from the perspective of the user (Manager), outlining
the actions they can perform and the information they can access. It
details the logical purpose of each functionality and the underlying
technical implementation .

\textbf{6.3.1 Dashboard Sections}

These sections represent the different types of analysis results and
information that the Manager can consult or view through the Store
Analysis Dashboard interface. The data
\end{quote}

78

\begin{quote}
Chapter 6. Sprint 3 - Deployment

and visualizations for these consultations are generated by the system's
backend processes, applying specific data analysis techniques and
calculations.

\textbf{6.3.1.1} \textbf{Heatmap Analysis}

\textbf{-- Logical Description:} The Manager needs to understand
customer traffic flow and identify areas within the store where
customers spend the most time. The heatmap provides a visual
representation of customer density across the store layout,
high-lighting hot spots and less visited areas. This information is
crucial for optimizing store layout, product placement, and promotional
displays.

\textbf{-- Data Analysis Perspective:}

∗\textbf{Input Data:} The primary input data for the heatmap analysis is
the collection of customer movement paths, which are sequences of
(\emph{x, y}) coordinates on the store map for each tracked person.

∗\textbf{Analytical Process:}

\textbf{1.Data Aggregation:} A 2D grid (self.heatmap) representing the
store map is used as an accumulator. For each point (\emph{x, y}) in
every customer path, the value at the corresponding grid cell (\emph{y,
x}) is incremented. This is a form of spatial aggregation, counting the
frequency of visits to each location.
\end{quote}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}@{}}
\toprule()
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
