person\_id
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
(\emph{x′,y′})\emph{∈}pathperson\_id
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\begin{quote}
I(\emph{x′}= \emph{x} and \emph{y′}= \emph{y})
\end{quote}
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule()
\endhead
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\begin{quote}
where I(\emph{·}) is the indicator function, equal to 1 if the condition
is true, and 0 otherwise.

\textbf{2.Spatial Smoothing:} To create a more visually intuitive
representation of density and reduce noise, a Gaussian filter is applied
to the raw heatmap data (gaussian\_filter(self.heatmap, sigma=20)). This
convolution operation effectively averages the counts in a neighborhood
around each pixel, weighted by a Gaussian function. The sigma parameter
controls the size of the neigh-borhood and the degree of smoothing.

\textbf{3.Normalization:} The smoothed heatmap is normalized to a range
between 0 and 1. This process scales the data proportionally, making it
suitable for mapping to a color gradient for visualization.

∗\textbf{Key Metrics/Outputs:} The primary output is the
normalized\_heatmap
\end{quote}

79

\begin{quote}
Chapter 6. Sprint 3 - Deployment

data, a 2D array where values represent the relative dwell time or
traffic density at each location.

∗\textbf{Presentation:} The normalized\_heatmap is visualized as an
image using matplotlib.pyplot.imshow with a chosen colormap (e.g., a
custom blue-to-red gradient). This image, along with an overlay showing
shelf zones, is saved as PNG files (store\_heatmap\_analysis.png,
large\_heatmap.png). The ReportGenerator includes these pre-generated
image files in the PDF re-port using reportlab.platypus.Image, allowing
the Manager to consult the visual heatmap analysis.

\textbf{6.3.1.2} \textbf{Shelf Performance}

\textbf{-- Logical Description:} The Manager needs to evaluate the
effectiveness of individ-ual shelves or product displays. This involves
analyzing customer interactions with specific shelf zones, quantifying
visitor traffic, engagement levels, and calculating performance metrics
to identify high-performing and underperforming areas.

\textbf{-- Data Analysis Perspective:}

∗\textbf{Input Data:} The input includes the defined shelf zones and the
detailed customer movement paths, along with the derived interaction
data (time spent near each shelf per person) stored in
self.person\_metrics.

∗\textbf{Analytical Process:}

\textbf{i.Interaction Detection and Quantification:} For each customer
path, the system determines when and for how long a customer is within
the defined interaction\_radius of each shelf zone. This time is
measured in frames. An interaction is classified as 'engaged' if the
time spent exceeds a threshold (5 frames in the code), otherwise
'passing'.

\textbf{ii.Shelf-Specific Metrics Calculation:} For each shelf, the
following metrics are calculated:

\textbf{Visitors:} The total number of unique people who entered the
interaction radius of the shelf at least once.

Visitorsshelf = \emph{\textbar\{}person\_id \emph{\textbar{} ∃}(\emph{x,
y}) \emph{∈}pathperson\_id\emph{,} dist((\emph{x, y})\emph{,}
centershelf) \emph{≤}radiusshelf

\textbf{Engaged Visitors:} The number of unique people who spent more
than
\end{quote}

80

\begin{quote}
Chapter 6. Sprint 3 - Deployment

the engagement threshold time within the shelf's interaction radius.

Engaged Visitorsshelf = \emph{\textbar\{}person\_id \emph{\textbar{}}
TimeSpentperson\_id\emph{,}shelf \emph{\textgreater{}}
threshold\emph{\}\textbar{}}

\textbf{Engagement Rate:} The proportion of visitors to a shelf who
became engaged visitors.
\end{quote}

Engagement Rateshelf =Engaged Visitors\uline{shelf} max(1\emph{,}
\uline{Visitors}shelf)

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Traffic Share:} The proportion of visitors to a shelf compared
to the total number of visitors who interacted with \emph{any} shelf in
the store.
