\textbf{6.3.1.3 People Tracking}

• \textbf{Logical Description:} The Manager needs overall statistics
about customer traffic vol-ume and the typical duration of customer
visits to the store. This provides a high-level view of store activity
and customer behavior patterns in terms of time spent.

• \textbf{Data Analysis Perspective:}

\textbf{-- Input Data:} The input data is the set of tracked individuals
and the duration of their presence within the store area. This comes
either from loaded self.people\_paths or live video analysis in
analyze\_person\_tracking.

\textbf{-- Analytical Process:}

\textbf{A.Active People Count:} The total number of unique individuals
identified and tracked during the analysis period is counted
(self.active\_people\_count). This is simply the number of distinct
person IDs.

\textbf{B.Time Spent Calculation:} For each tracked individual, the
total time they spent within the store area is calculated. If using
paths, this is based on path length and frame rate (e.g., len(path) /
fps). If using live tracking, it's accumulated over time in
analyze\_person\_tracking. This data is stored in self.time\_spent\_data
as a dictionary mapping person IDs to their total time spent.

\textbf{C.Average Time Spent:} The average time spent per customer is
calculated by summing the time spent by all individuals and dividing by
the total number of active people.
\end{quote}

Average Time Spent =

\begin{quote}
person\_idTimeSpentperson\_id max(1\emph{,} Active People Count)

\textbf{D.Time Spent Distribution:} The individual time spent data
(self.time\_spent\_data) represents a distribution. Statistical analysis
can involve calculating percentiles (e.g., 25th, 50th, 75th, 90th
percentiles) to understand the range and typical values of visit
durations.
\end{quote}

82

\begin{quote}
Chapter 6. Sprint 3 - Deployment

\textbf{-- Key Metrics/Outputs:} The outputs include the active people
count, average time spent, and the detailed time\_spent\_data for each
individual.

\textbf{-- Presentation:} The active\_people\_count and avg\_time\_spent
are included in\\
the interaction\_summary.json file. A histogram visualization of the
time spent\\
data distribution (time\_spent\_distribution.png) is generated using
matplotlib.pyplot.hi The ReportGenerator loads the
interaction\_summary.json and includes the time\\
spent distribution image and presents the key metrics and calculated
percentiles as\\
formatted text in the PDF report, allowing the Manager to consult these
traffic and\\
duration insights.

\textbf{6.3.1.4 Demography}

• \textbf{Logical Description:} The Manager may wish to understand the
demographic character-istics of the store's visitors (gender
distribution) to tailor marketing campaigns, product offerings, and
store environment to the primary customer base.

• \textbf{Data Analysis Perspective:}

\textbf{-- Input Data:}

∗The input data is video frames, from which individual persons are
detected and tracked.

\textbf{-- Analytical Process:}

∗\textbf{Person Detection and Tracking:} A YOLO model (`best.pt`) is
used to detect persons in each video frame. Supervision's ByteTrack is
then employed to track unique individuals across frames, assigning a
unique ID to each person.

∗\textbf{Gender Classification:} For each tracked person, their image
ROI is sent to a dedicated gender classification YOLO model (`best.pt`).
The model predicts the gender (e.g., "Male", "Female") along with a
confidence score. This clas-sification is performed either upon a
person's first appearance or periodically (every 30 frames) for existing
tracks to refine the gender identification.

∗\textbf{Gender Information Storage:} The identified gender, its
confidence, and the frames a person was first and last seen, along with
total frames tracked, are stored in tracked genders dictionary, mapped
by ID.

∗\textbf{Demographic Snapshots:} At regular intervals a snapshot of
currently active individuals (those seen within the last 60 frames) and
their gender distribution is saved to gender timeline.
\end{quote}

83

\begin{quote}
Chapter 6. Sprint 3 - Deployment

∗\textbf{Overall Gender Distribution Calculation:} After processing the
entire video, the final analysis method aggregates all recorded gender
information from tracked genders. It calculates the total number of
unique people ana-lyzed and the counts and percentages for each gender
category (Male, Female, Unknown).

∗\textbf{Average Time Spent by Gender:} The average time (in frames)
each gender spends in the analyzed area is calculated by summing the
total frames for all individuals of a specific gender and dividing by
the count of individuals for that gender.

\textbf{-- Key Metrics/Outputs:} The outputs include:

∗The total count of unique individuals detected and analyzed for gender.

∗A dictionary containing `counts` (absolute numbers) and `percentages`
for each gender category (Female, Male, Unknown).

∗The average number of frames (proxy for time) spent by customers of
each gender.

∗A chronological list of demographic snapshots, showing active people
and gen-der distribution over time.

∗A comprehensive record of each tracked person's gender, confidence, and
time-related data.

\textbf{-- Presentation:} The detailed demographic analysis is saved to
a json file in the specified data directory. A summary is also printed
to the console, showing the total people analyzed, the gender
distribution (counts and percentages), and the average time spent (in
frames) per gender. This provides a clear and concise overview for the
Manager.

