\textbf{Technical Implementation:}

\textbf{Logic:} This button click logically triggers a sequence of data
processing and analysis steps within the system.

\textbf{Technical Details:}

\textbf{--} The process begins by loading the necessary raw data,
including the perspective transformation matrix, customer movement
paths, and the store map image, from designated storage locations.

\textbf{--} The system then automatically identifies and defines the
specific areas on the store map that correspond to product shelves or
display zones.

\textbf{--} Customer movement paths are analyzed against these
identified shelf zones to detect and quantify interactions, determining
where customers spent time and for how long. This analysis also
contributes to building a spatial density map (heatmap) of customer
presence.

\textbf{--} Key performance indicators (KPIs) are calculated for each
shelf based on the de-tected interactions, such as visitor counts,
engagement levels, and a composite per-formance score.

\textbf{--} Visual representations of the analysis results are
generated, including smoothed heatmaps showing traffic density and
images overlaying shelf metrics onto the store map.

\textbf{--} A structured summary of the key findings and calculated
metrics is compiled.

\textbf{--} Finally, this summary data may be transmitted to an external
dashboard system for real-time display and monitoring.

\textbf{--} Logically, this single button click orchestrates the entire
analytical workflow from raw data input to processed insights and
generated outputs.

\textbf{6.3.2.2} \textbf{Generating report button}

\textbf{-- Logical Description:} This action allows the Manager to
generate and download a comprehensive PDF report summarizing the store
analytics. It provides a static
\end{quote}

85

\begin{quote}
Chapter 6. Sprint 3 - Deployment

document containing visualizations, key metrics, and analysis findings
for offline review, sharing, or archival purposes.

\textbf{-- Technical Implementation:}

∗\textbf{Logic:} The system must retrieve the results from the analysis
process and programatically assemble them into a formatted PDF document.

∗\textbf{Technical Details:}

\textbf{1.Data Retrieval:} The process starts by loading the previously
generated analysis results, including the summary data (overall metrics,
people tracking details) and the detailed shelf performance metrics,
from their storage files.

\textbf{2.Document Structure Setup:} A new PDF document is initiated
using a dedicated reporting library. Basic document properties such as
page size, margins, and the output file path are configured.

\textbf{3.Content Assembly:} The content of the report is built
sequentially. This involves adding various elements to the document's
structure:

Report title, generation date, and an executive summary are added as
formatted text paragraphs.

Section headings are included to organize the report into logical parts
(e.g., Heatmap Analysis, Shelf Performance, People Tracking).

Pre-generated visualization images (such as the heatmaps and time spent
distribution histogram) are embedded into the document, often with
de-scriptive captions.

Structured data, like the shelf performance metrics, is presented in
tables with defined formatting and styles.

Additional textual analysis and key metrics, extracted from the loaded
summary data (e.g., active people count, average time spent, time spent
percentiles), are included as formatted paragraphs or lists.

Spacing elements are added to improve the visual layout and readability
between different content blocks.

\textbf{4.PDF Generation:} The assembled content structure is processed
by the reporting library, which renders all the elements onto the PDF
pages according to their layout and styles, and writes the complete PDF
document to the specified output file.
\end{quote}

86

\begin{quote}
Chapter 6. Sprint 3 - Deployment

\textbf{5.Output:} Upon successful completion, the system provides the
filename of the generated PDF report, making it available for the user
to download.

